\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


Write $\mathbb D=\{z\in\mathbb C:|z|<1\}$ and $\mathbb T=\partial\mathbb D$. Let $S$ consist of holomorphic injective functions $f(z)=z+\sum_{n\ge2}a_nz^n$ on $\mathbb D$; set $a_0=0$, $a_1=1$. A function in $S$ is starlike if its image is starlike about $0$, and convex if its image is a convex domain.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For every starlike $f\in S$, is $\big||a_{n+1}|-|a_n|\big|\le1$ for all integers $n\ge1$? \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Historical question: can successive coefficient moduli of a starlike univalent function differ by more than one? The source reports Leung’s affirmative bound.

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 2306046 (AMR-022-6046). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] W. K. Hayman and E. F. Lingham, Research Problems in Function Theory (2018), arXiv:1809.07200, Chapter 6, Problem 6.46, complete update and preceding notation. \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200}.
